 # Regularization losses
 
-Also called: Regularization terms, Regularization objectives
+Also called: Regularization terms, Priors, Constraint losses
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization losses are auxiliary objective functions added to the total training loss of differentiable volume rendering to constrain the solution space, prevent overfitting, and enforce physical or geometric priors [S1, unofficial][S4]. They solve the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering by penalizing undesirable properties such as high-frequency noise, geometric ambiguity, or physically implausible density distributions [S1, unofficial]. This family is distinguished from photometric reconstruction losses (e.g., [Photometric Reconstruction Loss](7799-photometric-reconstruction-loss.md)) by targeting the internal consistency of the scene representation or the statistical properties of the rendered output rather than pixel-wise color fidelity.
+Regularization losses are auxiliary objective functions added to the total optimization loss in differentiable volume rendering to guide the solution toward physically plausible, structurally stable, or perceptually high-quality results, addressing the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering problems. Unlike photometric reconstruction losses that enforce fidelity to observed data, regularization losses enforce priors such as spatial smoothness, geometric compactness, or uncertainty bounds, distinguishing them from sibling methods like [Photometric Reconstruction Loss](7799-photometric-reconstruction-loss.md) which focus solely on pixel-wise error minimization.
 
